--- title Coordinate pairs
--- about A pair is two decimals written with an x between them. The
--- about spelling suggests two integers and R3 does not agree: FIRST 1x2
--- about is 1.0, and 1.5x2 is a legal pair. Everything here was asked of
--- about R3 3.22.1 rather than reasoned out, because the integer reading
--- about is the one you arrive at by thinking about it.

--- id pairs/reads-and-molds-back
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold 1x2
--- result
"1x2"

--- id pairs/reports-its-datatype
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
type? 1x2
--- result
#(pair!)

--- id pairs/first-half-is-a-decimal
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The whole reason this file exists. Nothing about the spelling
--- note 1x2 warns you that taking a half out gives you 1.0.
--- code
first 1x2
--- result
1.0

--- id pairs/second-half-is-a-decimal
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
second 1x2
--- result
2.0

--- id pairs/a-half-may-be-fractional
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Which is the other half of the same fact: if the halves were
--- note integers this would not read at all.
--- code
mold 1.5x2
--- result
"1.5x2"

--- id pairs/molding-hides-a-whole-half
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note So the decimal nature is invisible until you take a half out.
--- code
mold 1x2 * 1.5
--- result
"1.5x3"

--- id pairs/named-halves-read-through-a-path
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
p: 1x2
p/x
--- result
1.0

--- id pairs/second-named-half
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
p: 1x2
p/y
--- result
2.0

--- id pairs/halves-answer-to-their-positions-too
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note p/x and p/1 are the same question, which is worth pinning
--- note because only one of the two spellings is obvious.
--- code
p: 1x2
p/1
--- result
1.0

--- id pairs/picking-a-half-by-position
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
pick 1x2 1
--- result
1.0

--- id pairs/negative-halves-read
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The minus binds to each half separately, so this is one pair
--- note and not a subtraction.
--- code
mold -1x-2
--- result
"-1x-2"

--- id pairs/zero-pair
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold 0x0
--- result
"0x0"

--- id pairs/addition-works-on-each-half
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold 1x2 + 3x4
--- result
"4x6"

--- id pairs/subtraction-works-on-each-half
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold 1x2 - 3x4
--- result
"-2x-2"

--- id pairs/multiplication-works-on-each-half
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold 1x2 * 2x3
--- result
"2x6"

--- id pairs/a-single-number-spreads-across-both-halves
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note This is the only place in the language where an operand is
--- note spread rather than widened to meet the other side.
--- code
mold 1x2 * 2
--- result
"2x4"

--- id pairs/adding-a-single-number-spreads-too
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold 1x2 + 1
--- result
"2x3"

--- id pairs/division-can-produce-a-fractional-half
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note And here the decimal halves become visible without anyone
--- note asking for them.
--- code
mold 1x2 / 2
--- result
"0.5x1"

--- id pairs/dividing-by-a-pair-divides-each-half
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold 3x4 / 2x2
--- result
"1.5x2"

--- id pairs/negate-flips-both-halves
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold negate 1x2
--- result
"-1x-2"

--- id pairs/equal-pairs
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
1x2 = 1x2
--- result
#(true)

--- id pairs/one-differing-half-is-enough
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
1x2 = 1x3
--- result
#(false)

--- id pairs/less-than-needs-both-halves
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
1x2 < 2x3
--- result
#(true)

--- id pairs/greater-than-is-not-the-negation-of-less-than
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Both halves must agree, so a pair that beats another on one
--- note axis and loses on the other is neither less nor greater. Code
--- note that sorts pairs cannot lean on trichotomy.
--- code
1x2 > 2x3
--- result
#(false)

--- id pairs/made-from-a-block-of-two
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold make pair! [1 2]
--- result
"1x2"

--- id pairs/converted-from-a-block-of-two
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold to pair! [3 4]
--- result
"3x4"

--- id pairs/converted-from-one-number-fills-both-halves
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold to pair! 5
--- result
"5x5"

--- id pairs/converted-to-a-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
to string! 1x2
--- result
"1x2"

--- id pairs/converted-to-a-block-wraps-rather-than-splits
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Which is not what you would guess for a two-part value.
--- code
mold to block! 1x2
--- result
"[1x2]"

--- id pairs/reverse-swaps-the-halves
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold reverse 1x2
--- result
"2x1"

--- id pairs/min-takes-the-smaller-of-each-half-separately
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note So the answer can be a pair that neither argument was.
--- code
mold min 1x2 2x1
--- result
"1x1"

--- id pairs/max-takes-the-larger-of-each-half-separately
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold max 1x2 2x1
--- result
"2x2"

--- id pairs/even-asks-about-both-halves
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
even? 2x2
--- result
#(true)

--- id pairs/odd-asks-about-both-halves
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
odd? 1x1
--- result
#(true)

--- id pairs/negative-asks-about-both-halves
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
negative? -1x-1
--- result
#(true)

--- id pairs/positive-asks-about-both-halves
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
positive? 1x1
--- result
#(true)

--- id pairs/a-pair-is-not-a-series
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Which settles what a pair is: two numbers in one value, not a
--- note two-item collection. LENGTH? refuses it outright.
--- code
length? 1x2
--- error script expect-arg

--- id pairs/lerp-walks-between-two-pairs
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note LERP is Oldes's, not R3-Alpha's. Halfway between the origin
--- note and 10x10 is 5x5.
--- code
mold lerp 0x0 10x10 0.5
--- result
"5x5"

--- id pairs/lerp-at-the-start
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold lerp 0x0 10x10 0.0
--- result
"0x0"

--- id pairs/lerp-at-the-end
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold lerp 0x0 10x10 1.0
--- result
"10x10"

--- id pairs/asking-whether-something-is-a-pair
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
pair? 1x2
--- result
#(true)

--- id pairs/converting-from-one-item-is-refused
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note A pair needs both halves. One below the count it wants is the
--- note interesting boundary, and R3 refuses rather than filling in.
--- code
to pair! [1]
--- error script bad-make-arg

--- id pairs/converting-from-three-items-is-refused
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
to pair! [1 2 3]
--- error script bad-make-arg

--- id pairs/converting-from-a-string-reads-it
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note So the string goes through the reader rather than being
--- note rejected as the wrong shape.
--- code
mold to pair! "1x2"
--- result
"1x2"

--- id pairs/converting-from-nothing-is-refused
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
to pair! none
--- error script bad-make-arg

--- id pairs/making-from-one-number-fills-both-halves
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold make pair! 5
--- result
"5x5"

--- id pairs/bitwise-and-of-two-pairs
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The bitwise operators work on the halves as whole numbers,
--- note which is the one place the decimal halves are ignored.
--- code
mold 1x2 and 3x3
--- result
"1x2"

--- id pairs/bitwise-or-of-two-pairs
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold 1x2 or 3x3
--- result
"3x3"

--- id pairs/bitwise-exclusive-or-of-two-pairs
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold 1x2 xor 3x3
--- result
"2x1"

--- id pairs/a-pair-never-equals-a-single-number
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
1x2 = 1
--- result
#(false)

--- id pairs/even-needs-both-halves-even
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note One even half and one odd is the boundary that separates
--- note "asks about both" from "asks about either".
--- code
even? 1x2
--- result
#(false)

--- id pairs/odd-needs-both-halves-odd
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
odd? 1x2
--- result
#(false)

--- id pairs/negative-needs-both-halves-negative
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
negative? -1x1
--- result
#(false)

--- id pairs/positive-needs-both-halves-positive
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
positive? 1x-1
--- result
#(false)

--- id pairs/arithmetic-with-a-non-number-is-refused
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
1x2 + none
--- error script expect-arg

--- id pairs/dividing-a-pair-by-zero-still-raises
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Spreading a single operand across both halves does not turn
--- note the division into a floating one that quietly gives infinity.
--- code
1x2 / 0
--- error math zero-divide

--- id pairs/absolute-applies-to-each-half
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold absolute -1x-2
--- result
"1x2"

--- id pairs/rounding-applies-to-each-half
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold round 1.5x2.5
--- result
"2x3"

--- id pairs/less-than-or-equal-accepts-two-equal-pairs
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
1x2 <= 1x2
--- result
#(true)

--- id pairs/greater-than-or-equal-accepts-two-equal-pairs
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
1x2 >= 1x2
--- result
#(true)

--- id pairs/not-equal-notices-one-differing-half
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
1x2 <> 1x3
--- result
#(true)
